      "editorial": {
        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "The draw is the argument of the program rather than rarity. Hope and Lando put violin and piano in a small room and move through a turn-of-the-century Europe already absorbing and reflecting new musical identities: Enescu's cosmopolitanism, Ravel's explicit Blues movement, Elgar's salon lyricism and Franck's long-form dialogue. As the first half of Hope's two-part Gilded Age idea at The Barns, it gives the later American-facing program a useful musical starting point instead of functioning as a generic recital.",
